---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { guidesBySlug } from '../../data/guides';

const g = guidesBySlug['review-ai-agent-json'];
const path = `/guides/${g.slug}`;

const REPORT = `{
  "exitCode": 1,
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "/work/config.json",
      "opened": true,
      "saved": true,
      "valid": false,
      "errorCount": 1,
      "errors": [{ "pointer": "/retries", "message": "\\"3\\" is not of type \\"integer\\"" }],
      "problem": null
    }
  ]
}`;

const AGENT_NOTE = `## JSON review
Before writing a JSON file, hand it to me in Pandia:
  cat draft.json | pandia --wait - > draft.reviewed.json && mv draft.reviewed.json <target>
The command waits until I close the tab. Exit 3 means I closed it without
saving: stop and ask me what to change. If a schema exists, add --schema <path>.`;

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'Does my JSON leave my machine?',
    a: 'No. Pandia runs locally and opens what the agent pipes in from a temporary file that only your user account can read. The file is deleted when you close the tab.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which AI agents does this work with?',
    a: 'Any agent that can run a shell command: Claude Code, Cursor, Codex CLI, Aider and others. They all call the same pandia command.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens if I close the tab without saving?',
    a: 'Nothing is sent back. The command prints nothing and exits with code 3, so a script or agent that checks the exit code stops instead of writing an empty file.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can the agent fix schema errors on its own?',
    a: 'Yes. With --json, the command prints a report that lists each schema error with a JSON Pointer to the bad value, which the agent can read, fix and run again.',
  },
];
---
<GuideLayout
  title={g.title}
  description={g.description}
  path={path}
  heading={g.cardTitle}
  datePublished={g.datePublished}
  faqItems={faqItems}
>
  <h1>Review JSON from AI agents before it lands</h1>
  <p class="lead">Coding agents write config files, fixtures and API payloads in seconds. Here's how to check
  that JSON in Pandia before it reaches your project, with the agent waiting for your answer.</p>

  <p>An agent that writes JSON is fast and usually right. When it's wrong, the mistake is small and easy to
  miss: a number written as a string, a key that doesn't exist, a field from last year's API. Reading 400 lines
  of JSON in a chat window is not a review. You want the data in a proper viewer, checked against a schema, and
  a way to fix it before the agent moves on.</p>
  <p>The <code>pandia</code> command does that. The agent hands the JSON to Pandia and waits. You read it in the
  Tree, Grid or Code view, fix what's wrong, and press Save. The agent gets your corrected JSON back. Nothing
  leaves your machine.</p>

  <h2>Set up the command</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><a href="/download">Download Pandia</a> and open it.</li>
    <li>Open <strong>Settings → Behavior → terminal command</strong> and click
    <strong>Install pandia command</strong>.</li>
    <li>In a new terminal, run <code>pandia --help</code> to check it works.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The agent runs commands in your terminal, so once <code>pandia</code> works there, it works for the agent.
  See the <a href="/docs/command-line">command-line docs</a> for every option.</p>

  <h2>1. Review JSON before it's written</h2>
  <p>Ask the agent to pipe its draft into Pandia instead of writing the file directly:</p>
  <pre><code><span class="k">cat</span> draft.json | <span class="k">pandia</span> --wait - > config.next.json && <span class="k">mv</span> config.next.json config.json</code></pre>
  <p>Pandia opens the draft in a tab named <strong>stdin</strong>, with a bar saying a terminal is waiting.
  Edit anything you like, press <strong>Save</strong> (<kbd>⌘S</kbd>) to send it back, then close the tab. The
  command prints the JSON you saved, and <code>mv</code> puts it in place.</p>
  <p>If you close the tab without saving, nothing is sent back and the command exits with code 3, so
  <code>&&</code> stops before <code>mv</code>. That's why the command writes to <code>config.next.json</code>
  first: <code>></code> empties its target as soon as the command starts, so redirecting straight into
  <code>config.json</code> would leave it blank.</p>

  <h2>2. Check it against a schema</h2>
  <p>If the file has a JSON Schema, add <code>--schema</code>. The Schema panel shows every error while you
  review, and the exit code tells the agent whether the result passed:</p>
  <pre><code><span class="k">cat</span> draft.json | <span class="k">pandia</span> --wait - --schema config.schema.json \
  > config.next.json && <span class="k">mv</span> config.next.json config.json</code></pre>
  <p>A document with a top-level <code>$schema</code> loads its schema on its own; <code>--schema</code> wins
  when both are present. Exit codes with <code>--wait</code>:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>0</td><td>You closed the tabs and the Schema panel showed no errors.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>The Schema panel showed errors, a file didn't open, or Pandia quit before you closed the tabs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>The command was mistyped.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3</td><td>Piped input was closed without saving, so nothing was sent back.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>3. Let the agent read the result</h2>
  <p>Add <code>--json</code> and the command prints one JSON report when you close the tabs, instead of the
  document. The agent can read it, fix each error and ask you again:</p>
  <pre><code><span class="k">pandia</span> --wait --json config.json --schema config.schema.json</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{REPORT}</code></pre>
  <p>Each error names its location as a JSON Pointer, so <code>/retries</code> tells the agent exactly which
  value to change. <code>valid</code> is <code>null</code> when no schema ran, and <code>problem</code> says
  why a file didn't open.</p>

  <h2>4. Review changes to an existing file</h2>
  <p>When the agent edits a file you already have, ask it to write the new version next to the old one and
  open both in Compare:</p>
  <pre><code><span class="k">pandia</span> --wait --diff config.json config.new.json</code></pre>
  <p>Compare matches the two documents by structure, not by line. Keys in a different order are not a change,
  and an array item that moved shows as one move, not as hundreds of changed lines. Close the tab when you're
  done, and tell the agent whether to keep the new version.</p>

  <h2>5. Work with files too big for the agent</h2>
  <p>A multi-gigabyte log export or API dump doesn't fit in an agent's context. Pandia
  <a href="/guides/open-large-json-file">opens it instantly</a>, so you can do the looking:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Open it with <code>pandia events.json</code>.</li>
    <li>Find what matters in the Tree, or filter the array in the Grid.</li>
    <li>Right-click a value and choose <strong>Copy → Path</strong> for its exact location, such as
    <code>$.items[3].status</code>, or <strong>Copy → Value</strong> for its JSON.</li>
    <li>Paste just that into the agent instead of the whole file.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Tell your agent to do this every time</h2>
  <p>Most agents read a project instructions file, such as <code>AGENTS.md</code> or <code>CLAUDE.md</code>. Add
  a short note there so the agent asks for review on its own:</p>
  <pre><code>{AGENT_NOTE}</code></pre>
  <p>The command waits until you close the tab, which can take minutes. If your agent stops commands that run
  long, raise its time limit for this one.</p>

  <h2>Chat review or Pandia review</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Task</th><th>Reading it in the chat</th><th>Reviewing in Pandia</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Spot a wrong type or missing field</td><td>By eye</td><td>Schema panel lists each one</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fix a value before it's written</td><td>Ask the agent again</td><td>Edit it and press Save</td></tr>
      <tr><td>See what changed in an existing file</td><td>A text diff</td><td>A structural Compare</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Files over a few MB</td><td>Too big to paste</td><td>Opens any size</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data stays on your machine</td><td>Depends on the agent</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {faqItems.map((f) => (
    <>
      <h3>{f.q}</h3>
      <p>{f.a}</p>
    </>
  ))}

  <p class="lp-related">Related: <a href="/docs/command-line">command-line docs</a> ·
  <a href="/docs/validate">validate against a schema</a> ·
  <a href="/guides/open-large-json-file">open a large JSON file</a> · <a href="/download">download Pandia</a>.</p>
</GuideLayout>
